crow another time and he brought it home, too and tamed it. It's named Soot because

15
00:01:16,319 --> 00:01:23,800
it's so black and it hops and flies about with him everywhere." The time had come when Mary

16
00:01:23,800 --> 00:01:29,198
had forgotten to resent Martha's familiar talk. She had even begun to find

17
00:01:29,198 --> 00:01:34,198
it interesting and to be sorry when she stopped or went

18
00:01:34,198 --> 00:01:39,359
away. The stories she had been told by her Aya when she lived in India had been

19
00:01:39,359 --> 00:01:44,639
quite unlike those Martha had to tell about the Morland cottage which held 14

20
00:01:44,639 --> 00:01:50,600
people who lived in four little rooms and never had quite enough to eat. The

21
00:01:50,600 --> 00:01:57,079
children seemed to tumble about and amuse themselves like a litter of rough, good-natured collie

22
00:01:57,079 --> 00:02:02,319
puppies. Mary was most attracted by the mother and Dickon. When Martha told

23
00:02:02,319 --> 00:02:06,840
stories of what "mother" said or did they always sounded

24
00:02:06,840 --> 00:02:12,560
comfortable. "If I had a raven or a fox cuup I could play with it," said Mary. "But

25
00:02:12,560 --> 00:02:19,639
I have nothing." Martha looked perplexed. "Can that knit?" she

26
00:02:19,639 --> 00:02:30,319
asked. "No," answered Mary. "Can th so No." "Can thou read?"

27
00:02:30,319 --> 00:02:36,079
"Yes." "Then why doesn't thou read something or learn a bit of spelling thst old

28
00:02:36,079 --> 00:02:41,679
enough to be learning thy book a good bit now." "I haven't any books," said Mary.

29
00:02:41,679 --> 00:02:47,800
"Those I had were left in India." "That's a pity," said Martha. "If Mrs

30
00:02:47,800 --> 00:02:52,679
Medlock let thee go into the library, there's thousands of books there." Mary

31
00:02:52,679 --> 00:02:56,039
did not ask where the library was, because she was sudden ly inspired by a

32
00:02:56,039 --> 00:03:01,198
new idea. She made up her mind to go and find it herself. She was not troubled

33
00:03:01,198 --> 00:03:07,399
about Mrs. Medlock. Mrs. Medlock seemed always to be in her comfortable housekeeper sitting room

34
00:03:07,399 --> 00:03:14,560
downstairs In this queer place one scarcely ever saw anyone at all. In fact,

35
00:03:14,560 --> 00:03:19,319
there was no one to see but the servants, and when their master was away they

36
00:03:19,319 --> 00:03:24,560
lived a luxurious life below stairs, where there was a huge kitchen hung

37
00:03:24,560 --> 00:03:29,520
about with shining brass and putor and a large servants' hall where there were

38
00:03:29,520 --> 00:03:34,639
four or five abundant meals eaten every day, and where a great deal of lively

39
00:03:34,639 --> 00:03:41,840
romping went on when Mrs. Medlock was out of the way. Mary's meals were served regularly,

40
00:03:41,840 --> 00:03:46,800
and Martha waited on her, but no one troubled themselves about her in the

41
00:03:46,800 --> 00:03:52,000
least. Mrs. Medlock came and looked at her every day or two, but no one inquired

42
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:56,719
what she did or told her what to do. She supposed that perhaps this was the

43
00:03:56,719 --> 00:04:00,760
English way of treating children. In India she had always been

44
00:04:00,760 --> 00:04:06,439
attended by her Aya who had followed her about and waited on her, hand and foot.

45
00:04:06,439 --> 00:04:09,039
She had often been tired of her company.

46
00:04:09,039 --> 00:04:13,600
Now she was followed by nobody and was learning to dress herself because Martha

47
00:04:13,600 --> 00:04:17,359
looked as though she thought she was silly and stupid when she wanted to have

48
00:04:17,359 --> 00:04:24,840
things handed to her and put on. "Hasn't that got good sense?" she said once, when

49
00:04:24,840 --> 00:04:29,800
Mary had stood waiting for her to put on her gloves for her. "Our Susan Anne is

50
00:04:29,800 --> 00:04:37,719
twice as sharp as thee and she's only 4 year' old. Sometimes th looks fair soft in the

51
00:04:37,719 --> 00:04:43,238
head." Mary had worn her contrary scowl for an hour after that, but it made her

52
00:04:43,238 --> 00:04:49,000
think several entirely new things. She stood at the window for about

53
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:53,880
10 minutes this morning after Martha had swept up the hearth for the last time

54
00:04:53,880 --> 00:04:58,118
and gone downstairs She was thinking over the new idea which

55
00:04:58,118 --> 00:05:03,639
had come to her when she heard of the library. She did not care very much about

56
00:05:03,639 --> 00:05:10,238
the library itself, because she had read very few books; but to hear of it brought back to

57
00:05:10,238 --> 00:05:16,840
her mind the hundred rooms with closed doors. She wondered if they were all

58
00:05:16,840 --> 00:05:22,760
really locked and what she would find if she could get into any of them. Were

59
00:05:22,760 --> 00:05:28,000
there a hundred really? Why shouldn't she go and see how many doors she could

60
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:32,560
count? It would be something to do on this morning when she could not go

61
00:05:32,560 --> 00:05:37,600
out. She had never been taught to ask permission to do things, and she knew

62
00:05:37,600 --> 00:05:42,118
nothing at all about authority, so she would not have thought it necessary to

63
00:05:42,118 --> 00:05:47,920
ask Mrs. Medlock if she might walk about the house, even if she had seen

64
00:05:47,920 --> 00:05:54,158
her. She opened the door of the room and went into the corridor, and then she

65
00:05:54,158 --> 00:06:01,960
began her her wanderings. It was a long corridor and it branched into other corridors and it led

66
00:06:01,960 --> 00:06:09,399
her up short flights of steps which mounted to others again. There were doors and doors, and

67
00:06:09,399 --> 00:06:13,679
there were pictures on the walls. Sometimes they were pictures of

68
00:06:13,679 --> 00:06:19,719
dark, curious landscapes, but oftenest they were portraits of men and women in

69
00:06:19,719 --> 00:06:26,118
queer, grand costumes made of satin and velvet. She found found herself in one

70
00:06:26,118 --> 00:06:31,238
long gallery whose walls were covered with these portraits. She had never

71
00:06:31,238 --> 00:06:37,639
thought there could be so many in any house. She walked slowly down this place

72
00:06:37,639 --> 00:06:43,359
and stared at the faces which also seemed to stare at her. She felt as if

73
00:06:43,359 --> 00:06:48,439
they were wondering what a little girl from India was doing in their house. Some

74
00:06:48,439 --> 00:06:53,639
were pictures of children— —little girls in thick satin frocks which reached to

75
00:06:53,639 --> 00:06:58,399
their feet and stood out about them, and boys with puffed sleeves and lace

76
00:06:58,399 --> 00:07:04,238
collars and long hair, or with big roughs around their necks. She always stopped to

77
00:07:04,238 --> 00:07:09,198
look at the children, and wonder what their names were, and where they had gone,

78
00:07:09,198 --> 00:07:15,880
and why they wore such odd clothes. There was a stiff, plain little girl rather like

79
00:07:15,880 --> 00:07:22,359
herself. She wore a green bade dress and held a green parrot on her finger. Her

80
00:07:22,359 --> 00:07:25,639
eyes had a sharp, curious look.

81
00:07:25,639 --> 00:07:31,118
"Where do you live now?" said Mary aloud to her. "I wish you were

82
00:07:31,118 --> 00:07:37,198
here." Surely no other little girl ever spent such a queer morning. It seemed as

83
00:07:37,198 --> 00:07:42,359
if there was no one in all the huge rambling house but her own small self,

84
00:07:42,359 --> 00:07:47,840
wandering about upstairs and down, through narrow passages and wide ones,

85
00:07:47,840 --> 00:07:52,679
where it seemed to her that no one but herself had ever

86
00:07:52,679 --> 00:07:58,840
walked. Since so many rooms had been built, people must have lived in them, but

87
00:07:58,840 --> 00:08:03,679
it all seemed so empty that she could not quite believe it

88
00:08:03,679 --> 00:08:08,920
true. It was not until she climbed to the second floor that she thought of turning

89
00:08:08,920 --> 00:08:15,479
the handle of a door. All the doors were shut, as Mrs. Medlock had said they were,

90
00:08:15,479 --> 00:08:19,960
but at last she put her hand on the handle of one of them and turned

91
00:08:19,960 --> 00:08:24,719
it. She was almost frightened for a moment when she felt that it turned

92
00:08:24,719 --> 00:08:31,639
without difficulty and that when she pushed upon the door itself it slowly and heavily

93
00:08:31,639 --> 00:08:39,238
opened. It was a massive door and opened into a big bedroom. There were embroidered hangings

94
00:08:39,238 --> 00:08:45,439
on the wall, and inlaid furniture such as she had seen in India stood about the

95
00:08:45,439 --> 00:08:51,158
room. A broad window with leaded panes looked out upon the Moore and over the

96
00:08:51,158 --> 00:08:55,639
mantle was another portrait of the stiff, plain little girl who seemed to stare at

97
00:08:55,639 --> 00:09:01,800
her more curiously than ever. "Perhaps she slept here once," said

98
00:09:01,800 --> 00:09:09,118
Mary. "She stares at me so that she makes me feel queer." After that she opened more

99
00:09:09,118 --> 00:09:15,279
doors and more. She saw so many rooms that she became quite tired and began to

100
00:09:15,279 --> 00:09:19,920
think that there must be a hundred, though she had not counted them. In all

101
00:09:19,920 --> 00:09:25,158
of them there were old pictures or old tapestries with strange scenes worked on

102
00:09:25,158 --> 00:09:30,359
them. There were curious pieces of furniture and curious ornaments in

103
00:09:30,359 --> 00:09:36,719
nearly all of them. In one room, which looked like a lady's sitting room the

104
00:09:36,719 --> 00:09:42,679
hangings were all embroidered velvet, and in a cabinet were about a hundred little

105
00:09:42,679 --> 00:09:48,719
elephants made of ivory. They were of different sizes, and some had their mots

106
00:09:48,719 --> 00:09:53,719
or penquins on their backs. Some were much bigger than the others and some

107
00:09:53,719 --> 00:09:59,520
were so tiny that they seemed only babies. Mary had seen carved ivory in

108
00:09:59,520 --> 00:10:04,399
India and she knew all about elephants. She opened the door of the

109
00:10:04,399 --> 00:10:08,639
cabinet and stood on a foot stol and played with these for quite a long

110
00:10:08,639 --> 00:10:15,000
time. When she got tired she set the elephants in order and shut the door of the

111
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:20,719
cabinet. In all her wanderings through the long corridors in the empty rooms,

112
00:10:20,719 --> 00:10:26,158
she had seen nothing alive; but in this room room she saw

113
00:10:26,158 --> 00:10:32,719
something. Just after she had closed the cabinet door she heard a tiny rustling

114
00:10:32,719 --> 00:10:37,439
sound. It made her jump and look around at the sofa by the

115
00:10:37,439 --> 00:10:40,920
fireplace, from which it seemed to come.

116
00:10:40,920 --> 00:10:45,399
In the corner of the sofa there was a cushion, and in the velvet which covered

117
00:10:45,399 --> 00:10:51,439
it there was a hole, and out of the hole peeped a tiny head with a pair of

118
00:10:51,439 --> 00:10:58,279
frightened eyes in it. Mary crept softly across the room to look. The bright eyes

119
00:10:58,279 --> 00:11:03,760
belonged to a little gray mouse, and the mouse had eaten a hole into the cushion

120
00:11:03,760 --> 00:11:09,800
and made a comfortable nest there. Six baby mice were cuddled up asleep near

121
00:11:09,800 --> 00:11:15,800
her. If there was no one else alive in the hundred rooms there were seven mice

122
00:11:15,800 --> 00:11:20,760
who did not look lonely at all. "If they wouldn't be so frightened I

123
00:11:20,760 --> 00:11:24,719
would take them back with me," said Mary.

124
00:11:24,719 --> 00:11:29,760
She had wandered about long enough to feel too tired to wander any farther, and

125
00:11:29,760 --> 00:11:35,520
she turned back. Two or three times she lost her way by turning down the wrong

126
00:11:35,520 --> 00:11:40,840
corridor and was obliged to ramble up and down until she found the right one;

127
00:11:40,840 --> 00:11:45,439
but at last she reached her own floor again, though she was some distance from

128
00:11:45,439 --> 00:11:52,079
her own room and did not know exactly where she was. "I believe I have taken a wrong

129
00:11:52,079 --> 00:11:57,639
turning again," she said, standing still at what seemed the end of a short passage

130
00:11:57,639 --> 00:12:05,198
with tapestry on the wall. "I don't know which way to go. How still everything is!"

131
00:12:05,198 --> 00:12:09,279
It was while she was standing here and just after she had said this that the

132
00:12:09,279 --> 00:12:15,359
stillness was broken by a sound. It was another cry, but not quite like the one

133
00:12:15,359 --> 00:12:21,800
she had heard last night; it was only a short one, a fretful, childish wine

134
00:12:21,800 --> 00:12:25,000
muffled by passing through walls.

135
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:33,599
"It's nearer than it was," said Mary, her heart beating rather faster. "And it is

136
00:12:33,880 --> 00:12:38,800
crying." She put her hand accidentally upon the tapestry near her, and then

137
00:12:38,800 --> 00:12:43,359
sprang back, feeling quite startled. The tapestry was the covering of a door

138
00:12:43,359 --> 00:12:46,439
which fell open and showed her that there was another part of the corridor

139
00:12:46,439 --> 00:12:51,479
behind it, and Mrs. Medlock was coming up it with her bunch of keys in her hand

140
00:12:51,479 --> 00:12:54,920
and a very cross look on her face.

141
00:12:54,920 --> 00:13:01,238
"What are you doing here?" she said, and she took Mary by the arm and pulled her away.

142
00:13:01,238 --> 00:13:06,920
"What did I tell you?" "I turned around the wrong corner," explained Mary. "I didn't

143
00:13:06,920 --> 00:13:12,920
know which way to go and I heard someone crying." She quite hated Mrs. Medlock at

144
00:13:12,920 --> 00:13:17,880
the moment, but she hated her more the next. "You didn't hear anything of the

145
00:13:17,880 --> 00:13:24,960
sort," said the housekeeper. "You come along back to your own nursery or I'll box your ears."

146
00:13:24,960 --> 00:13:30,238
And she took her by the arm and half pushed, half pulled her up one passage

147
00:13:30,238 --> 00:13:35,238
and down another until she pushed her in at the door of her own

148
00:13:35,238 --> 00:13:41,880
room. "Now," she said, "you stay where you're told to stay or you'll find yourself

149
00:13:41,880 --> 00:13:47,520
locked up. The master had better get you a governor same as he said he would.

150
00:13:47,520 --> 00:13:53,319
You're one that needs someone to look sharp after you. I've got enough to do."

151
00:13:53,319 --> 00:13:57,800
She went out of the room and slammed the door after her, and Mary went and sat on

152
00:13:57,800 --> 00:14:04,520
the Hearth rug pale with rage. She did not cry, but ground her teeth. "There was

153
00:14:04,520 --> 00:14:11,039
someone crying— —there was —there was she said to herself. She had heard it twice

154
00:14:11,039 --> 00:14:16,679
now, and sometime she would find out. She had found out a great deal this morning.

155
00:14:16,679 --> 00:14:21,198
She felt as if she had been on a long journey, and at any rate she had had

156
00:14:21,198 --> 00:14:26,880
something to amuse her all the time, and she had played with the ivory elephants

157
00:14:26,880 --> 00:14:33,479
and had seen the gray mouse and its babies in their nest in the velvet

158
00:14:33,880 --> 00:14:34,000
cushion. CHAPTER 7 THE KEY OF THE
